  "Novelist and poet; the author of The Well of Loneliness, the most important lesbian novel in English.",
  "Born Marguerite Radclyffe Hall at Bournemouth on 12 August 1880; she published The Forge (1924), Adam's Breed (1926, James Tait Black Prize) and The Well of Loneliness (1928), which was prosecuted for obscenity in a celebrated trial. She lived with Una Troubridge and died in London on 7 October 1943.",
  "Radclyffe Hall wrote The Well of Loneliness, the most important lesbian novel in English and one of the most consequential books of the century: its prosecution for obscenity made it a cause celebre, and its portrait of Stephen Gordon gave the lesbian subject its first full novel and the modern literature of sexual identity its founding text.",
  "The full case history of the lesbian heroine; the plain, earnest, sympathetic prose; the argument by sympathetic portrait; the war service and the Paris salon; the final appeal to God and to justice.",
  "Sexuality, the new psychology; the woman question, married disability, and the New Woman; the First World War and the myth of sacrifice; art versus life, and the romance of the artist.",
  "Radclyffe Hall wrote The Well of Loneliness, the most important lesbian novel in English: its prosecution for obscenity made it a cause celebre, and its portrait of Stephen Gordon gave the lesbian subject its first full novel and founded the modern literature of sexual identity.",
